| "Install dependencies" step, median | {{ caches.install_median_s | int }} seconds |
| "Install dependencies" step, 90th percentile | {{ caches.install_p90_s | int }} seconds |

Query: `python -m cidiag query caches`. Source: `GET /repos/{{ config.repo }}/actions/jobs/{id}/logs`, filtered to the cache and test-count lines.

The cache key includes a hash of the lock file, so a miss is expected on the first run after a dependency update. With a {{ caches.install_median_s | int }}-second median install there's no time to recover here.

The cache list on {{ config.as_of }} held {{ caches.entries }} entries totaling {{ caches.entries_bytes | mb }} MB. {{ caches.entries_never_read }} had never been read since they were written, {{ caches.entries_never_read_on_pr }} of them on pull-request refs, where a cache written by one pull request can't be read by another. That's how GitHub scopes caches and isn't a fault. It's worth knowing because a repository's cache storage is capped (10 GB by default), and entries nobody reads count toward the cap.

Nothing to change here.

## 6. The top change, tested on a fork

**The change.** Add `parallel: '--parallel'` to three of the four `test-mysql` matrix entries (MySQL 8.0, MariaDB 10.6 and MySQL 8.4). The MariaDB 11.4 entry stays serial.

The workflow file explains why some jobs are serial: an error raised inside a parallel test process sometimes can't be passed back, and the traceback is lost, so the project keeps one serial run for each database. Today all four `test-mysql` jobs are serial. This change keeps one. We kept the MariaDB 11.4 job because it runs on Python 3.14 and is the fastest of the four. Whether one serial job is enough for MySQL and MariaDB together, or whether each needs its own, is the project's call. With two kept serial the saving in runner minutes would be smaller, and the saving in waiting would depend on which two.

**How it was tested.** We forked the repository to [{{ fix.repo }}](https://github.com/{{ fix.repo }}) and pushed two branches from commit `{{ static.ref | sha }}`:

- `{{ fix.arms.before.branch }}`: the pipeline as it is.
- `{{ fix.arms.after.branch }}`: the same, plus the three lines.

GitHub didn't run the workflows our fork inherited, and they're enabled from the fork's web interface, which we didn't use. Both branches carry a copy of `test.yml` under a new name, `ci-diagnostic.yml`. The copy differs from the original in its name, its triggers, and a condition that stops our fork from uploading coverage to Codecov. The jobs are identical. Runs were started one at a time, each after the previous one finished, so no run competed with another for runners. The two branches were interleaved over the same few hours; the table below gives each run's start time. Nothing was pushed to {{ config.repo }} and no pull request was opened there.

